  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 345 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nLuckily, this cold snap will be brief, as surface winds will back \naround to return flow Sunday evening, with a respectable recovery \nMonday as flow aloft transitions to zonal in the wake of the trough. \nBy Monday afternoon, temperatures should be back up to ~ 6-8 F above \nnormal.   \n\nTuesday, an upper trough will approach the west coast, making \nlandfall at SoCal by 00Z Wednesday and transitioning zonal flow over \nthe area to southwest.  Thicknesses will peak Tuesday afternoon, \nwith highs topping out in the 70s most locations.  Wednesday, the \nwest coast trough opens, and traverses east, almost to the Four \nCorners by 00Z Thursday.  Thicknesses will begin decreasing, and a \nsort of Pac front will push into the area Wednesday afternoon.  Long-\nterm models are still in pretty good agreement in developing showers \nalong and behind this feature, and POPs have increased slightly \nWednesday night from runs 24 hours ago.  This appears to be the only \nwindow for rain this forecast, and QPF looks tepid, should it pan \nout.\n\nThe upper trough will move through the area Thursday, dropping a \nweak front in its wake Thursday night.  Highs Friday afternoon \nshould close out the extended right around normal for this time of \nyear.",
